(function(){window.PhysicaRankPyqNeet_physics_motioninastraightline=[{id:"neet-2026-q005",pn:5,y:"NEET 2026 \xB7 3 May",tp:"Motion in a Straight Line",st:"Velocity-time graphs",q:"The following plots show variation of velocity (v) with time (t), of a ball thrown vertically upward, and falling back. Which of the following plots is/are correct ?",o:["C only","A and E only","D only","B only"],c:0,s:`During the whole journey the acceleration due to gravity is vertically downward.
So the slope of the velocity-time curve must be negative throughout the journey.
Therefore only statement (C) is correct.`,fig:{w:215,h:375,alt:"Five velocity-time plots A to E for a ball thrown up and falling back",d:[["l",40,70,40,18,"a"],["l",40,66,190,66,"a"],["t",35,26,"v","e"],["t",34,70,"0","e"],["t",195,70,"t"],["t",12,70,"A."],["p","M40 28L76 66L116 28"],["l",40,138,40,86,"a"],["l",40,134,190,134,"a"],["t",35,94,"v","e"],["t",34,138,"0","e"],["t",195,138,"t"],["t",12,138,"B."],["p","M44 134L64 102L86 134"],["l",40,232,40,154,"a"],["l",40,202,190,202,"a"],["t",35,162,"v","e"],["t",34,206,"0","e"],["t",195,206,"t"],["t",12,206,"C."],["l",40,160,106,226],["l",40,274,40,222,"a"],["l",40,270,190,270,"a"],["t",35,230,"v","e"],["t",34,274,"0","e"],["t",195,274,"t"],["t",12,274,"D."],["l",40,270,106,232],["l",40,342,40,290,"a"],["l",40,338,190,338,"a"],["t",35,298,"v","e"],["t",34,342,"0","e"],["t",195,342,"t"],["t",12,342,"E."],["p","M40 300Q62 338 80 338T130 298"]]},sfig:{w:200,h:110,alt:"Velocity-time graph: straight line from positive v sloping down across the t axis",d:[["l",40,92,40,10,"a"],["l",40,50,170,50,"a"],["t",35,18,"v","e"],["t",34,54,"0","e"],["t",175,54,"t"],["l",40,18,120,80]]}},{id:"neet-2026r-q020",pn:20,y:"Re-NEET 2026 \xB7 21 Jun",tp:"Motion in a Straight Line",st:"Average speed",q:"Consider a particle moving along a straight line, whose position as a function of time is given by s(t) = \u03B1t\xB2 \u2013 \u03B2t + \u03B3, where \u03B1 = 1 ms\u207B\xB2, \u03B2 = 6 ms\u207B\xB9 and \u03B3 = 5 m. The average speed of the particle, in ms\u207B\xB9 from t = 0 to t = 6 s is:",o:["6","3","0","12"],c:1,s:`Position s = \u03B1t\xB2 \u2212 \u03B2t + \u03B3 = t\xB2 \u2212 6t + 5 \u2026(i).
Velocity v = ds/dt = 2t \u2212 6 \u2026(ii).
v = 0 gives 2t \u2212 6 = 0, so t = 3 s (the particle turns back).
In the v-t graph, v goes from \u22126 at t = 0 to 0 at t = 3 and to +6 at t = 6.
Distance d = A\u2081 + A\u2082 = (1/2) \xD7 3 \xD7 6 + (1/2) \xD7 3 \xD7 6.
d = 9 + 9 = 18 m.
Average speed = distance/time = 18/6 = 3 ms\u207B\xB9.`,sfig:{w:290,h:215,alt:"v-t graph: a straight line from v = -6 at t = 0 through zero at t = 3 to v = 6 at t = 6; area A1 below the axis for 0 to 3 and area A2 above it for 3 to 6.",d:[["l",33,203,33,28,"b"],["l",33,118,270,118,"a"],["l",33,178,138,57],["l",33,57,138,57,"d"],["l",138,57,138,118,"d"],["t",38,26,"v","s"],["t",26,61,"6","e s"],["t",26,122,"0","e s"],["t",26,184,"-6","e s"],["t",274,122,"t","s"],["t",85,134,"3","m s"],["t",138,134,"6","m s"],["t",42,136,"A\u2081","s"],["t",112,104,"A\u2082","s"]]}},{id:"neet-2026-q030",pn:30,y:"NEET 2026 \xB7 3 May",tp:"Motion in a Straight Line",st:"Free fall",q:`When a ruler falls vertically, 5 different persons catch it with different reaction times. (g = 9.8 m s\u207B\xB2)
A. Person A has reaction time of 0.20 s.
B. Person B has reaction time of 0.22 s.
C. Person C has reaction time of 0.18 s.
D. Person D has reaction time of 0.19 s.
E. Person E has reaction time of 0.21 s.
What is the correct order of the distance travelled by the ruler for each person?`,o:["C > D > A > B > E","C > D > A > E > B","B > E > A > C > D","B > E > A > D > C"],c:3,s:`The ruler falls from rest, so the distance grows with the reaction time (S = \xBDgt\xB2).
A larger reaction time gives a larger distance.
Descending order of reaction time: t_B > t_E > t_A > t_D > t_C.
So descending order of distance covered: S_B > S_E > S_A > S_D > S_C.`},{id:"neet-2025-q015",pn:15,y:"NEET 2025",tp:"Motion in a Straight Line",st:"Acceleration from position-time relation",q:"In some appropriate units, time (t) and position (x) relation of a moving particle is given by t = x\xB2 + x. The acceleration of the particle is",o:["\u22122/(x+2)\xB3","\u22122/(2x+1)\xB3","+2/(x+1)\xB3","+2/(2x+1)"],c:1,s:`t = x\xB2 + x
dt/dx = 2x + 1
v = dx/dt = 1/(2x + 1)
dv/dx = \u22122/(2x + 1)\xB2
a = v(dv/dx) = [1/(2x + 1)] \xD7 [\u22122/(2x + 1)\xB2]
a = \u22122/(2x + 1)\xB3`},{id:"neet-2025-q021",pn:21,y:"NEET 2025",tp:"Motion in a Straight Line",st:"Relative velocity",q:"Two cities X and Y are connected by a regular bus service with a bus leaving in either direction every T min. A girl is driving scooty with a speed of 60 km/h in the direction X to Y notices that a bus goes past her every 30 minutes in the direction of her motion, and every 10 minutes in the opposite direction. Choose the correct option for the period T of the bus service and the speed (assumed constant) of the buses.",o:["9 min, 40 km/h","25 min, 100 km/h","10 min, 90 km/h","15 min, 120 km/h"],c:3,s:`Direction X \u2192 Y (same as the scooty):
Let the velocity of the bus = v km/h.
Relative velocity of bus w.r.t. scooty = (v \u2212 60)
Distance between 2 consecutive buses = vT
(v \u2212 60) \xD7 30 = vT ...(i)
Direction Y \u2192 X (opposite):
(v + 60) \xD7 10 = vT ...(ii)
Equating (i) and (ii): (v \u2212 60)30 = (v + 60)10
v = 120 km/h
T = 15 min`,sfig:{w:260,h:75,alt:"Two dots X on the left and Y on the right; a dot (scooty) between them with an arrow to the right labelled v_scooty = 60 km/h.",d:[["c",13.5,32.4,3.2,"F"],["t",14.4,19.8,"X","m"],["c",89.1,32.4,3.2,"F"],["l",94.5,32.4,137.7,32.4,"a"],["c",220.5,32.4,3.2,"F"],["t",221.4,19.8,"Y","m"],["t",85.5,59.4,"v",""],["t",92.7,63,"scooty","s"],["t",129.6,59.4,"= 60 km/h",""]]}},{id:"neet-2024-q047",pn:47,y:"NEET 2024 \xB7 5 May",tp:"Motion in a Straight Line",st:"Acceleration-time from v-t graph",q:`The velocity (v) \u2013 time (t) plot of the motion of a body is shown below:
The acceleration (a) \u2013 time (t) graph that best suits this motion is:`,o:["(graph 1)","(graph 2)","(graph 3)","(graph 4)"],c:0,s:`At the start the body has zero velocity and zero slope, so the acceleration is zero.
Then the v-t slope is constant and positive, so the acceleration is a constant positive value.
After some time the velocity becomes constant, so the acceleration is zero.
Then the v-t slope is constant and negative, so the acceleration is a constant negative value.
So the a-t graph is a positive rectangular pulse, a gap at zero, then a negative rectangular pulse.`,fig:{w:345,h:450,alt:"A v-t graph in which the velocity rises linearly from zero, stays constant, then falls linearly to zero; below it four a-t graphs (1) to (4): (1) a positive rectangular pulse then a negative rectangular pulse, (2) a negative pulse then a rising sloped line, (3) a positive trapezoid bump, (4) a rising line to a constant positive level.",d:[["l",60,100,60,22,"a"],["l",60,100,215,100,"a"],["p","M85 100 L95 58 L145 58 L175 100"],["t",60,14,"v (m s\u207B\xB9)","m"],["t",215,118,"t (s)","e"],["t",54,114,"0","e"],["l",40,160,40,280,"b"],["l",28,240,160,240,"a"],["t",40,152,"a (m s\u207B\xB2)","m"],["t",160,254,"t (s)","e"],["t",35,253,"0","e"],["t",2,244,"(1)"],["p","M62 240 V218 H82 V240"],["p","M98 240 V265 H120 V240"],["l",215,160,215,280,"b"],["l",203,240,335,240,"a"],["t",215,152,"a (m s\u207B\xB2)","m"],["t",335,254,"t (s)","e"],["t",210,253,"0","e"],["t",177,244,"(2)"],["p","M248 240 V262 H287 V240"],["l",287,240,307,221],["l",40,320,40,440,"b"],["l",28,400,160,400,"a"],["t",40,312,"a (m s\u207B\xB2)","m"],["t",160,414,"t (s)","e"],["t",35,413,"0","e"],["t",2,404,"(3)"],["p","M50 400 L57 378 H85 L100 400"],["l",215,320,215,440,"b"],["l",203,400,335,400,"a"],["t",215,312,"a (m s\u207B\xB2)","m"],["t",335,414,"t (s)","e"],["t",210,413,"0","e"],["t",177,404,"(4)"],["p","M248 400 L270 330 H298"]]},sfig:{w:170,h:90,alt:"An a-t graph with a positive rectangular pulse, a gap at zero, then a negative rectangular pulse.",d:[["l",40,75,40,3,"a"],["l",18,45,150,45,"a"],["t",32,12,"a","e"],["t",155,60,"t","e"],["p","M56 45 V25 H76 V45"],["p","M93 45 V67 H113 V45"]]}},{id:"neet-2023-q001",pn:1,y:"NEET 2023 \xB7 7 May",tp:"Motion in a Straight Line",st:"Average speed",q:"A vehicle travels half the distance with speed v and the remaining distance with speed 2v. Its average speed is:",o:["3v/4","v/3","2v/3","4v/3"],c:3,s:`Equal distances are covered at two speeds v\u2081 and v\u2082.
So average speed = 2v\u2081v\u2082/(v\u2081 + v\u2082).
Put v\u2081 = v and v\u2082 = 2v.
Average speed = (2 \xD7 v \xD7 2v)/(v + 2v) = 4v\xB2/3v = 4v/3.`},{id:"neet-2023-q040",pn:40,y:"NEET 2023 \xB7 7 May",tp:"Motion in a Straight Line",st:"Motion under gravity",q:"A horizontal bridge is built across a river. A student standing on the bridge throws a small ball vertically upwards with a velocity 4 m s\u207B\xB9. The ball strikes the water surface after 4 s. The height of bridge above water surface is (Take g = 10 m s\u207B\xB2)",o:["68 m","56 m","60 m","64 m"],c:3,s:`Take upward as positive. Use s = ut \u2212 \xBDgt\xB2.
s = 4\xD74 \u2212 \xBD\xD710\xD716 = 16 \u2212 80 = \u221264 m.
The ball ends 64 m below the start, so the bridge is 64 m above the water.`,sfig:{w:260,h:150,alt:"A person on a horizontal bridge throws a ball up at 4 m/s; the dotted path rises and falls to the water; h is the height of the bridge above the water.",d:[["l",10,70,130,70],["l",10,132,150,132],["c",70,38,5,""],["l",70,43,70,58],["l",70,47,62,55],["l",70,47,80,42],["l",70,58,64,70],["l",70,58,76,70],["c",82,36,2.5,"F"],["l",86,50,86,14,"a"],["t",78,12,"4 m/s","e"],["p","M88 14C96 -2 140 0 140 40V132","d"],["l",104,72,104,130,"b"],["t",112,106,"h"]]}},{id:"neet-2023-q049",pn:49,y:"NEET 2023 \xB7 7 May",tp:"Motion in a Straight Line",st:"Motion with constant retardation",q:"A bullet from a gun is fired on a rectangular wooden block with velocity u. When bullet travels 24 cm through the block along its length horizontally, velocity of bullet becomes u/3. Then it further penetrates into the block in the same direction before coming to rest exactly at the other end of the block. The total length of the block is :",o:["30 cm","27 cm","24 cm","28 cm"],c:1,s:`Use v\xB2 = u\xB2 \u2212 2as with constant retardation a.
First 24 cm: (u/3)\xB2 = u\xB2 \u2212 2a(24), so 2a(24) = 8u\xB2/9.
Next part s: 0 = (u/3)\xB2 \u2212 2as, so 2as = u\xB2/9.
Dividing, s/24 = (u\xB2/9)/(8u\xB2/9) = 1/8, so s = 3 cm.
Total length = 24 + 3 = 27 cm.`},{id:"neet-2022-q005",pn:5,y:"NEET 2022 \xB7 17 Jul",tp:"Motion in a Straight Line",st:"Distance in nth second",q:"The ratio of the distances travelled by a freely falling body in the 1st, 2nd, 3rd and 4th second",o:["1 : 4 : 9 : 16","1 : 3 : 5 : 7","1 : 1 : 1 : 1","1 : 2 : 3 : 4"],c:1,s:`Distance in the nth second from rest is S = (a/2)(2n \u2013 1), with a = g.
n = 1: (g/2)(1).
n = 2: (g/2)(3).
n = 3: (g/2)(5).
n = 4: (g/2)(7).
Ratio = 1 : 3 : 5 : 7.`},{id:"neet-2022-q033",pn:33,y:"NEET 2022 \xB7 17 Jul",tp:"Motion in a Straight Line",st:"Slope of displacement-time graph",q:"The displacement-time graphs of two moving particles make angles of 30\xB0 and 45\xB0 with the x-axis as shown in the figure. The ratio of their respective velocity is",o:["1 : 1","1 : 2","1 : \u221A3","\u221A3 : 1"],c:2,s:`The slope of an x-t graph gives the velocity.
Ratio = tan 30\xB0/tan 45\xB0 = (1/\u221A3)/1.
So the ratio is 1 : \u221A3.`,fig:{w:210,h:160,alt:"Displacement-time graph with two straight lines from the origin at 30 degrees and 45 degrees to the time axis, marked with arcs.",d:[["l",30,130,30,12,"a"],["l",30,130,200,130,"a"],["t",38,12,"displacement"],["t",196,148,"time","e"],["t",20,144,"0","e"],["l",30,130,160,55],["l",30,130,115,45],["p","M60 130A30 30 0 0 0 56 115"],["p","M92 130A62 62 0 0 0 73.8 86.2"],["t",64,124,"30\xB0","s"],["t",96,124,"45\xB0","s"]]}},{id:"neet-2022r-q043",pn:43,y:"Re-NEET 2022 \xB7 4 Sep",tp:"Motion in a Straight Line",st:"x-t graph for acceleration",q:"The position-time (x-t) graph for positive acceleration is:",o:["(1)","(2)","(3)","(4)"],c:1,s:`Positive acceleration means dv/dt > 0, so velocity increases.
Velocity is the slope of the x-t graph, so the slope must keep increasing.
That is a curve bending upward (convex), as in option (2).`,fig:{w:480,h:105,alt:"Four position-time graphs: (1) straight line falling to the t axis, (2) curve starting flat and rising ever more steeply, (3) curve rising steeply then flattening, (4) straight line rising at constant slope.",d:[["t",4,55,"(1)"],["l",34,90,34,15,"a"],["l",34,90,116,90,"a"],["t",34,10,"x","m"],["t",120,94,"t"],["l",34,28,102,90],["t",114,55,"(2)"],["l",144,90,144,15,"a"],["l",144,90,226,90,"a"],["t",144,10,"x","m"],["t",230,94,"t"],["p","M144 90Q177 90 210 30"],["t",224,55,"(3)"],["l",254,90,254,15,"a"],["l",254,90,336,90,"a"],["t",254,10,"x","m"],["t",340,94,"t"],["p","M254 90Q254 30 320 30"],["t",334,55,"(4)"],["l",364,90,364,15,"a"],["l",364,90,446,90,"a"],["t",364,10,"x","m"],["t",450,94,"t"],["l",364,90,432,30]]}},{id:"neet-2021-q018",pn:18,y:"NEET 2021 \xB7 12 Sep",tp:"Motion in a Straight Line",st:"Distance in nth second",q:"A small block slides down on a smooth inclined plane, starting from rest at time t = 0. Let Sn be the distance travelled by the block in the interval t = n \u2013 1 to t = n. Then, the ratio Sn/Sn+1 is-",o:["2n/(2n \u2013 1)","(2n \u2013 1)/2n","(2n \u2013 1)/(2n + 1)","(2n + 1)/(2n \u2013 1)"],c:2,s:`Acceleration down a smooth incline is a = g sin \u03B8 (constant), starting from rest.
Distance in the nth second: Sn = u + (a/2)(2n \u2013 1) = (a/2)(2n \u2013 1).
For the next second: Sn+1 = (a/2)(2n + 1).
Divide: Sn/Sn+1 = (2n \u2013 1)/(2n + 1).`},{id:"neet-2020-q114",pn:114,y:"NEET 2020 \xB7 13 Sep",tp:"Motion in a Straight Line",st:"Equations of motion",q:"A ball is thrown vertically downward with a velocity of 20 m/s from the top of a tower. It hits the ground after some time with a velocity of 80 m/s. The height of the tower is : (g = 10 m/s\xB2)",o:["340 m","320 m","300 m","360 m"],c:2,s:`Use v\xB2 = u\xB2 + 2gh.
80\xB2 = 20\xB2 + 2 \xD7 10 \xD7 h.
6400 = 400 + 20h.
h = 6000/20 = 300 m.`,sfig:{w:160,h:130,alt:"Ball thrown down from tower of height h: 20 m/s at the top, 80 m/s at the ground",d:[["l",10,15,22,15],["l",16,15,16,50],["t",12,66,"h"],["l",16,76,16,110],["l",10,110,22,110],["l",10,110,115,110],["c",60,15,3,"F"],["l",60,20,60,108,"da"],["t",72,19,"20 m/s"],["t",68,100,"80 m/s"]]}},{id:"neet-2018-q029",pn:29,y:"NEET 2018 \xB7 6 May",tp:"Motion in a Straight Line",st:"Average velocity and average speed",q:"A toy car with charge q moves on a frictionless horizontal plane surface under the influence of a uniform electric field E\u20D7. Due to the force qE\u20D7 its velocity increases from 0 to 6 m/s in one second duration. At that instant the direction of the field is reversed. The car continues to move for two more seconds under the influence of this field. The average velocity and the average speed of the toy car between 0 to 3 seconds are respectively",o:["2 m/s, 4 m/s","1 m/s, 3 m/s","1 m/s, 3.5 m/s","1.5 m/s, 3 m/s"],c:1,s:`First second: a = 6 m/s\xB2, distance = (1)/(2) \xD7 6 \xD7 1\xB2 = 3 m, forward.
Field reversed: acceleration \u20136 m/s\xB2. Speed 6 m/s drops to 0 in 1 s, distance 3 m forward.
Third second: starts at rest, accelerates backward, distance 3 m backward.
Total path = 3 + 3 + 3 = 9 m; net displacement = 3 + 3 \u2013 3 = 3 m.
Average velocity = (3)/(3) = 1 m/s; average speed = (9)/(3) = 3 m/s.`},{id:"neet-2017-q170",pn:170,y:"NEET 2017 \xB7 7 May",tp:"Motion in a Straight Line",st:"Relative velocity",q:"Preeti reached the metro station and found that the escalator was not working. She walked up the stationary escalator in time t1. On other days, if she remains stationary on the moving escalator, then the escalator takes her up in time t2. The time taken by her to walk up on the moving escalator will be :",o:["t1 \u2212 t2","(t1 + t2)/2","t1t2/(t2 \u2212 t1)","t1t2/(t2 + t1)"],c:3,s:`Let the length be d. Her walking speed is d/t1 and the escalator speed is d/t2.
Walking on the moving escalator, the speeds add: d/t = d/t1 + d/t2.
1/t = (t1 + t2)/(t1 t2), so t = t1t2/(t1 + t2).`}]})();
